# csq audit bundle — verification & trust notice

This bundle is a self-contained, zero-install export of a csq audit chain. It
contains the signed record chain, every public key needed to verify it, and a
pure-Python (`verify`) script that reproduces csq's canonical form and checks
every signature with NO csq installation and NO third-party packages.

## How to verify

```
tar xf <this-bundle>.tar
cd <extracted-dir>
./verify                    # or: python3 verify
```

Exit `0` = PASS (chain verified end-to-end). Any non-zero exit prints a `FAIL:`
line naming the specific integrity check that failed. Confirm the genesis public
key in `public_keys.json` out-of-band before trusting a PASS.

## Trust notice — these attestations are HONEST-HOST GRADE

A PASS from `verify` proves that this bundle's records form an unbroken,
signature-valid chain under the genesis key in `public_keys.json`, and that no
file was added, removed, or altered after export — `BUNDLE.sig` signs
`BUNDLE.lock`, which hashes every file in the bundle, including this `README.md`,
and `verify` rejects any file not covered by `BUNDLE.lock`. This holds **only if
you confirmed that genesis public key out-of-band** (above): an attacker who
re-signs a tampered bundle with their own key and swaps `public_keys.json`
produces an identical PASS, so the out-of-band key check is what makes the PASS
meaningful.

It does **not**, on its own, prove that the csq instance that produced the chain
was itself uncompromised. Without corroboration by an **external witness** — an
independent transparency log (Rekor) or a Foundation notary that co-signs the
chain head — these records are **honest-host grade**: an auditor can verify that
csq governed the session exactly as recorded, but cannot prove csq was not
compromised at production time.

Do not treat a signature-valid record — whatever `verification_level` it carries
— as tamper-evident against a compromised host. It is tamper-evident **in
transit** (this bundle), not tamper-evident **at the source**, until an external
witness corroborates the chain head. The external witness is a portfolio-level
dependency; when it exists, the same records become tamper-evident and
independently verifiable.
